# Lesson 3, new exercise: the lesson's KQJ3 opposite small: two sure club tricks once the ace is gone, three on a good day.
title: Clubs on a good day
id: L03-20
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2
student: N
board: 52
dealer: W
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AQ5    K84    AK62   852
  E  J4     QJT97  T73    T94
  S  K72    A53    854    KQJ3
  W  T9863  62     QJ9    A76

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 9-11
  spades: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king. Dummy's ace stops the hearts a second time.
  diamonds: 2-3 = Two on top. A third if the opponents' six split 3-3.
  clubs: 2-3 = Two once the ace is gone. A third if they split 3-3.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Dummy's clubs have no winners on top. How many will they have once the ace is gone?
play:
  1   E HQ  S H3  W H2  N HK
      note: Seven winners on top: three spades, two hearts, two diamonds. Once the ♣A is gone, dummy's clubs give at least two more. Win in your hand and keep dummy's ♥A to stop the hearts again.
  2   N C2  E C4  S CK  W CA
      note: Start on clubs.
  3   W H6  N H4  E HJ  S HA
      note: Dummy's ♥A stops the hearts a second time.
  4   S CQ  W C7  N C5  E C9
      note: Dummy's clubs are winners now.
  5   S CJ  W C6  N C8  E CT
      note: Both opponents have followed three times...
  6   S C3  W S3  N H8  E H9
      note: ...so even dummy's ♣3 wins. A good day.
  7   S S2  W S6  N SQ  E S4
      note: Now the spades.
  8   N SA  E SJ  S S7  W S8
      note: Then the ace.
  9   N S5  E H7  S SK  W S9
      note: Over to dummy's ♠K.
  10  S D4  W D9  N DA  E D3
      note: And the diamonds.
  11  N DK  E D7  S D5  W DJ
      note: That's ten tricks.
  12  N D6  E DT  S D8  W DQ
      note: The rest belong to the defense.
  13  W ST  N D2  E HT  S H5
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count seven winners on top, then force out the ♣A to get two or
  three club tricks.

  You needed only two club tricks. The clubs split 3-3, so you got a third,
  and an overtrick.
